    "evidence_text": "Altering the phosphorylation state of each protein causes corresponding chromosome segregation defects.\n\nPersistent cyclin A expression prevents k-MT stabilization even in cells with aligned chromosomes.\n\nMicrotubule sliding activity of a kinesin-8 promotes spindle assembly and spindle length control\n\nHere, we report an antiparallel microtubule-sliding activity of the budding yeast kinesin-8, Kip3.\n\nIn conjunction with Cin8, a kinesin-5 family member, the sliding activity of Kip3 promotes bipolar spindle assembly and the maintenance of genome stability.\n\nWe propose a slide-disassemble model where the sliding and destabilizing activity of Kip3 balance during pre-anaphase.\n\nThis facilitates normal spindle assembly.\n\nPolo kinase and Asp are needed to promote the mitotic organizing activity of centrosomes\n\nKMN\u2013spindle attachment is regulated by the Aurora BIpl1 and MPS1Mps1 kinases.\n\nIt is unclear whether other mechanisms exist that support KMN activity during the cell cycle.\n\nThe aurora B kinase promotes inner and outer kinetochore interactions in budding yeast.\n\nThe kinetochore is the macromolecular protein complex that mediates chromosome segregation.",
